    "suggested_action": "replace_ja_expression",
    "reason": "The English lemma 'extra' (noun) can refer to any additional item or amount beyond what is usual or necessary. The Japanese expression '補用' (hoyou) specifically means 'for supplementary use' or 'for replacement/spare use,' often in a more technical or formal context (e.g., spare parts). While it covers the 'spare' aspect of 'extra,' it is too narrow for the general sense of 'extra' as an additional item in daily life. For example, one would not use '補用' for 'an extra slice of cake.' It is not the most natural or common term a general learner would expect for 'extra' in a broad daily life context.",
    "suggested_ja_replacement": {
      "surface": "予備",
      "reading": "よび",
      "reason": "「予備」 (yobi) is a common and natural noun meaning 'reserve,' 'spare,' or 'extra (item/amount kept in reserve).' It aligns well with the general sense of the English noun 'extra' in daily life contexts, covering both 'spare' and 'additional item kept in reserve,' and is much broader and more frequently used than '補用' for this concept."
